            '{"parser": "legacy-migration", "file_name": "סיכום למבחן אחזור מידע (1).pdf", "file_type": "pdf", "legacyData": {"legacy_id": "4ae8da0f-832d-460b-85bc-aa33c6053c15", "external_id": null, "collection": "collection", "type": "chunk-data", "tags": "", "user_id": "de0ff05457533c93fdf3e0d1cdd0f808", "create_date": "2025-08-26T11:29:00.372Z", "link_to_file": "https://jeenfilesdevweu.blob.core.windows.net/jeendocs/de0ff05457533c93fdf3e0d1cdd0f808/data/1756207659351-0c8cd1917d00e951630029e4978e9147.pdf", "excerptKeywords": "KEYWORDS: הסתברות, מאורע, קובייה, מספר זוגי, שופטים"}}'::jsonb,
            '2025-08-26T11:29:00.372000+00:00',
            $TRANS$The probability of event C is P(C) = 3/6 = 0.5 independent events What are the chances that we have tossed the dice twice and got an even number? We will record two events: • First event – A – On the first toss we received an even number. What is the probability? • Second event – B – On the second toss we were given an even number. What is the probability? Our assumption: A and B are independent. What does this mean? P (AՈB)=P(A) x P(B) Kappa In the example we have two judges, in our case there are 50 documents, and we will try to see how many cases they agreed and how many cases they did not. The table shows frequencies.$TRANS$
        );
