            NULL,
            436,
            77,
            '{"parser": "legacy-migration", "file_name": "סיכום למבחן אחזור מידע (1).pdf", "file_type": "pdf", "legacyData": {"legacy_id": "6ef1496b-ab81-4f34-b8a2-b446396918ed", "external_id": null, "collection": "collection", "type": "chunk-data", "tags": "", "user_id": "de0ff05457533c93fdf3e0d1cdd0f808", "create_date": "2025-08-26T11:29:00.286Z", "link_to_file": "https://jeenfilesdevweu.blob.core.windows.net/jeendocs/de0ff05457533c93fdf3e0d1cdd0f808/data/1756207659351-0c8cd1917d00e951630029e4978e9147.pdf", "excerptKeywords": "KEYWORDS: reave, trigram, bigram, permuterm index, k-gram index"}}'::jsonb,
            '2025-08-26T11:29:00.286000+00:00',
            $TRANS$We will pull out words for each term and cut between the lists. What the two lists have in common is the word reave. We will filter against the re*ve search template and indeed the word is appropriate. How do we determine N? N is determined by success. We take a document, divide it in half, use half to build the model and the other part to test our success. Typically, a trigram is used for a large dictionary that contains millions of records and a bigram for a smaller dictionary. Permuterm index via k-gram index • K-gram index – more efficient in terms of space.$TRANS$
